    "q-270847": {
      "dom_id": "step-q270847",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find the roots of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-9x+14}{{x}^{2}-4}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The roots of $f(x)$ are the solutions to the equation $f(x)=0.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}-9x+14}{{x}^{2}-4}=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-9x+14}{{x}^{2}-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-7)(x-2)}{(x+2)(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-7)\\cancel{(x-2)}}{(x+2)\\cancel{(x-2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-7}{x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the numerator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-7 & =0\\,⟹\\,x=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to $f(x)=0$ is $x=7.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
