    "q-113088": {
      "dom_id": "step-q113088",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Find the roots of $f(z)=\\frac{{z}^{2}+5z+4}{{z}^{2}+4z}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The roots of $f(z)$ are the solutions to the equation $f(z)=0.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{{z}^{2}+5z+4}{{z}^{2}+4z}=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(z) & =\\frac{{z}^{2}+5z+4}{{z}^{2}+4z} \\\\  & =\\frac{(z+1)(z+4)}{z(z+4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(z+1)\\cancel{(z+4)}}{z\\cancel{(z+4)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(z+1)}{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the numerator equal to zero and solve for $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+1 & =0\\,⟹\\,z=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to $f(z)=0$ is $z=-1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
