    "q-25299": {
      "dom_id": "step-q25299",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Find the roots of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-20}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-9x+18}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The roots of $f(x)$ are the solutions to the equation $f(x)=0.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-20}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-9x+18}=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+x-20}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-9x+18} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+5)(x-4)}{{x}^{2}(x-2)-9(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+5)(x-4)}{({x}^{2}-9)(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+5)(x-4)}{(x+3)(x-3)(x-2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the numerator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)(x-4)=0\\,⟹\\,x=-5,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to $f(x)=0$ are $x=-5$ and $x=4.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
