    "q-278034": {
      "dom_id": "step-q278034",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "In **ascending order**, the roots of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-x+2}{3x-3}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$\nand\n$\\,\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}",
      "worked_solution": "The roots of $f(x)$ are the solutions to the equation $f(x)=0.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-x+2}{3x-3}=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-x+2}{3x-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}(x-2)-1(x-2)}{3x-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}-1)(x-2)}{3(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-1)(x+1)(x-2)}{3(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(x-1)}(x+1)(x-2)}{3\\cancel{(x-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(x-2)}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the numerator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-1,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions to $f(x)=0$, in ascending order, are $x=-1$ and $x=2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
