    "q-25312": {
      "dom_id": "step-q25312",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Find the roots of $f(h)=\\frac{{h}^{2}+9h+20}{{h}^{3}+3{h}^{2}-25h-75}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The roots of $f(h)$ are the solutions to the equation $f(h)=0.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{{h}^{2}+9h+20}{{h}^{3}+3{h}^{2}-25h-75}=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(h) & =\\frac{{h}^{2}+9h+20}{{h}^{3}+3{h}^{2}-25h-75} \\\\  & =\\frac{(h+5)(h+4)}{{h}^{2}(h+3)-25(h+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(h+5)(h+4)}{({h}^{2}-25)(h+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(h+5)(h+4)}{(h+5)(h-5)(h+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(h+5)}(h+4)}{\\cancel{(h+5)}(h-5)(h+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{h+4}{(h-5)(h+3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the numerator equal to zero and solve for $h:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h+4=0\\,⟹\\,h=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to $f(h)=0$ is $h=-4.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
